# `rates::hull_white_zcb`

The closed-form Hull-White price of a zero-coupon bond for a linear reversion level.

## Signature

```cpp
double rates::hull_white_zcb(double maturity, double short_rate, const rates::HullWhiteParams &params);
```

## What It Does

The rate is Gaussian, so `P = exp(−E[∫r] + Var[∫r]/2)`, with `E∫₀^T r = r B + θ₀(T − B) + θ₁(T²/2 − (T − B)/κ)`, `B = (1 − e^{−κT})/κ` and `Var∫r = σ²/κ²·[(T − B) − κB²/2]` (the formula in the library is the exact Gaussian expression). With `theta_slope = 0` it equals the Vasicek price for the same `κ, θ₀, σ`.

## Parameters

`double maturity`
: Years to maturity, non-negative; zero returns 1.

`double short_rate`
: The current short rate.

`const rates::HullWhiteParams &params`
: The model.

## Returns

The price of a bond paying 1 at maturity.

## Errors

- A negative or non-finite maturity, a non-finite rate or invalid parameters throw (`pluss::Error`).

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

A term structure with a rising level.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace rt = fin::rates;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const rt::HullWhiteParams p{.kappa = 0.7, .sigma = 0.01, .r0 = 0.03, .theta0 = 0.03, .theta_slope = 0.004};
  for (const double T : {0.25, 1.0, 5.0, 10.0}) {
    const double price = rt::hull_white_zcb(T, 0.03, p);
    out.writeln("T", T, ": price", price, ", yield", -std::log(price) / T);
  }
}
```

### Example 2

With a flat level it equals the Vasicek price.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace rt = fin::rates;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  const rt::HullWhiteParams hw{.kappa = 0.7, .sigma = 0.01, .r0 = 0.05, .theta0 = 0.03, .theta_slope = 0.0};
  const rt::VasicekParams vas{.kappa = 0.7, .theta = 0.03, .sigma = 0.01, .r0 = 0.05};
  out.writeln("Hull-White", rt::hull_white_zcb(5.0, 0.05, hw), ", Vasicek", rt::vasicek_zcb(5.0, 0.05, vas));
}
```

### Example 3

Against simulation with a rising level.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace rt = fin::rates;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const rt::HullWhiteParams p{.kappa = 0.7, .sigma = 0.01, .r0 = 0.03, .theta0 = 0.03, .theta_slope = 0.004};
  rt::SimOptions options;
  options.scheme = rt::Scheme::Transition;
  options.seed = 3;
  const double dt = 1.0 / 252.0;
  const Series rate = rt::hull_white_paths(1260, 20000, dt, p, options).rate;
  const Series integral = rate.slice(0, 0, 1260, 1).sum(0, false) * dt;
  out.writeln("simulated", (integral * -1.0).exp().mean().item<double>(), ", closed form", rt::hull_white_zcb(5.0, 0.03, p));
}
```

